const e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"RC_E5_001","exercise":"exercise_5","passage_id":"RC_E5_P1","passage":"Soon after dawn on 11 May 1857, about 150 years ago, the Mughal Emperor Bahadur Shah Zafar was saying his morning prayers in his oratory overlooking the river Jumna when he saw a cloud of dust rising on the far side of the river. Minutes later, he was able to see its cause: 300 East India Company cavalrymen charging wildly towards his palace.\\n\\nThe troops had ridden overnight from Meerut, where they had turned their guns on their British officers and had come to Delhi to ask the emperor to give his blessing to their mutiny. As a letter sent out by the rebels' leaders subsequently put it: \\"The English are people who overthrow all religions ... As the English are the common enemy of both the Hindus and Muslims, we should unite in their slaughter ... By this alone will the lives and faiths of both be saved.\\"\\n\\nThe sepoys entered Delhi, massacred every Christian they could find, and declared the 82-year-old emperor to be their leader. Before long, the insurgency had snowballed into the largest and bloodiest anticolonial revolt against any European empire in the 19th century. Of the 139,000 sepoys of the Bengal army, all but 7,796 turned against the British. In many places the sepoys were supported by a widespread civilian rebellion.\\n\\nThere is much about British imperial adventures in the east at this time, and the massive insurgency it provoked, which is uneasily familiar to us today. The British had been trading in India since the early 17th century. But the commercial relationship changed towards the end of the 18th, as a new group of conservatives came to power in London, determined to make Britain the sole global power. Lord Wellesley, the brother of the Duke of Wellington and governor general in India from 1798 to 1805, called his new approach the Forward Policy. But it was in effect a project for a new British century. Wellesley made it clear he would not tolerate any European rivals, especially the French, and planned to remove any hostile Muslim regimes that might presume to resist the west's growing might.\\n\\nThe Forward Policy soon developed an evangelical flavour. The new conservatives wished to impose not only British laws but also western values on India. The country would be not only ruled but also redeemed. Local laws which offended Christian sensibilities were abrogated - the burning of widows, for instance, was banned. One of the East India Company directors, Charles Grant, spoke for many when he wrote of how he believed providence had brought the British to India for a higher purpose: \\"Is it not necessary to conclude that our Asiatic territories were given to us, not merely that we draw a profit from them, but that we might diffuse among their inhabitants, long sunk in darkness, the light of Truth?\\"\\n\\nThe British progressed from removing threatening Muslim rulers to annexing even the most pliant Islamic states. In February 1856, they marched into Avadh, also known by the British as Oudh. To support the annexation, a \\"dodgy dossier\\" was produced before parliament, so full of distortions and exaggerations that one British official who had been involved in the operation described the parliamentary blue book (or paper) on Oudh as \\"a fiction of official penmanship, an Oriental romance\\" that was refuted \\"by one simple and obstinate fact\\", that the conquered people of Avadh clearly \\"preferred the slandered regime\\" of the Nawab \\"to the grasping but rose-coloured government of the company\\".\\n\\nThe reaction to this came with the great mutiny, or as it is called in India, the first war of independence. Though it reflected many deeply held political and economic grievances, particularly the feeling that the heathen foreigners were interfering with a part of the world to which they were alien, the uprising was consistently articulated as a defensive action against the inroads missionaries and their ideas were making in India, combined with a generalised fight for freedom from western occupation.","question":"What strategic significance did the annexation of Avadh hold for the British in the context of their imperial ambitions in India?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"It provided access to key trade routes and resources vital for British economic interests.","B":"Avadh served as a stronghold for anti-British factions, necessitating its annexation for security reasons.","C":"The annexation strengthened British control over a strategically located region, facilitating further expansion.","D":"Avadh's annexation aimed to establish a model British administration to showcase imperial superiority."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"The annexation strengthened British control over a strategically located region, facilitating further expansion.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"contradicts_passage","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — the passage makes no mention of trade routes or economic resources in Avadh; this introduces a motive not stated in the text. Choice B is incorrect — the passage describes Avadh as one of the 'most pliant Islamic states', not a stronghold of anti-British resistance; this contradicts the passage. Choice C is CORRECT — the passage describes the annexation as part of the Forward Policy to consolidate British control over strategically important regions, with Avadh's central location making it key to further imperial expansion. Choice D is incorrect — the passage frames the annexation as territorial expansion, not as a demonstration of administrative superiority; this motive is absent from the text.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E5_002","exercise":"exercise_5","passage_id":"RC_E5_P1","passage":"Soon after dawn on 11 May 1857, about 150 years ago, the Mughal Emperor Bahadur Shah Zafar was saying his morning prayers in his oratory overlooking the river Jumna when he saw a cloud of dust rising on the far side of the river. Minutes later, he was able to see its cause: 300 East India Company cavalrymen charging wildly towards his palace.\\n\\nThe troops had ridden overnight from Meerut, where they had turned their guns on their British officers and had come to Delhi to ask the emperor to give his blessing to their mutiny. As a letter sent out by the rebels' leaders subsequently put it: \\"The English are people who overthrow all religions ... As the English are the common enemy of both the Hindus and Muslims, we should unite in their slaughter ... By this alone will the lives and faiths of both be saved.\\"\\n\\nThe sepoys entered Delhi, massacred every Christian they could find, and declared the 82-year-old emperor to be their leader. Before long, the insurgency had snowballed into the largest and bloodiest anticolonial revolt against any European empire in the 19th century. Of the 139,000 sepoys of the Bengal army, all but 7,796 turned against the British. In many places the sepoys were supported by a widespread civilian rebellion.\\n\\nThere is much about British imperial adventures in the east at this time, and the massive insurgency it provoked, which is uneasily familiar to us today. The British had been trading in India since the early 17th century. But the commercial relationship changed towards the end of the 18th, as a new group of conservatives came to power in London, determined to make Britain the sole global power. Lord Wellesley, the brother of the Duke of Wellington and governor general in India from 1798 to 1805, called his new approach the Forward Policy. But it was in effect a project for a new British century. Wellesley made it clear he would not tolerate any European rivals, especially the French, and planned to remove any hostile Muslim regimes that might presume to resist the west's growing might.\\n\\nThe Forward Policy soon developed an evangelical flavour. The new conservatives wished to impose not only British laws but also western values on India. The country would be not only ruled but also redeemed. Local laws which offended Christian sensibilities were abrogated - the burning of widows, for instance, was banned. One of the East India Company directors, Charles Grant, spoke for many when he wrote of how he believed providence had brought the British to India for a higher purpose: \\"Is it not necessary to conclude that our Asiatic territories were given to us, not merely that we draw a profit from them, but that we might diffuse among their inhabitants, long sunk in darkness, the light of Truth?\\"\\n\\nThe British progressed from removing threatening Muslim rulers to annexing even the most pliant Islamic states. In February 1856, they marched into Avadh, also known by the British as Oudh. To support the annexation, a \\"dodgy dossier\\" was produced before parliament, so full of distortions and exaggerations that one British official who had been involved in the operation described the parliamentary blue book (or paper) on Oudh as \\"a fiction of official penmanship, an Oriental romance\\" that was refuted \\"by one simple and obstinate fact\\", that the conquered people of Avadh clearly \\"preferred the slandered regime\\" of the Nawab \\"to the grasping but rose-coloured government of the company\\".\\n\\nThe reaction to this came with the great mutiny, or as it is called in India, the first war of independence. Though it reflected many deeply held political and economic grievances, particularly the feeling that the heathen foreigners were interfering with a part of the world to which they were alien, the uprising was consistently articulated as a defensive action against the inroads missionaries and their ideas were making in India, combined with a generalised fight for freedom from western occupation.","question":"How did the British East India Company's policies contribute to the underlying grievances that fuelled the Indian Rebellion of 1857?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"By implementing reforms aimed at addressing socio-economic inequalities and injustices.","B":"Through discriminatory practices that marginalized Indian cultural and religious norms.","C":"By promoting inclusive governance structures that empowered local communities.","D":"Through strategic alliances with indigenous leaders to resist external colonial pressures."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Through discriminatory practices that marginalized Indian cultural and religious norms.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","C":"contradicts_passage","D":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — the passage indicates Company policies exacerbated grievances rather than alleviating socio-economic disparities; this directly contradicts the passage. Choice B is CORRECT — the passage describes the Forward Policy's evangelical flavour, the imposition of western values, abrogation of local laws, and missionary activity as sources of resentment among Hindus and Muslims alike. Choice C is incorrect — the passage describes the Company as marginalising indigenous governance, not empowering it; this contradicts the passage. Choice D is incorrect — the passage describes the Company as undermining indigenous leadership rather than forming alliances with it; this contradicts the passage.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E5_003","exercise":"exercise_5","passage_id":"RC_E5_P1","passage":"Soon after dawn on 11 May 1857, about 150 years ago, the Mughal Emperor Bahadur Shah Zafar was saying his morning prayers in his oratory overlooking the river Jumna when he saw a cloud of dust rising on the far side of the river. Minutes later, he was able to see its cause: 300 East India Company cavalrymen charging wildly towards his palace.\\n\\nThe troops had ridden overnight from Meerut, where they had turned their guns on their British officers and had come to Delhi to ask the emperor to give his blessing to their mutiny. As a letter sent out by the rebels' leaders subsequently put it: \\"The English are people who overthrow all religions ... As the English are the common enemy of both the Hindus and Muslims, we should unite in their slaughter ... By this alone will the lives and faiths of both be saved.\\"\\n\\nThe sepoys entered Delhi, massacred every Christian they could find, and declared the 82-year-old emperor to be their leader. Before long, the insurgency had snowballed into the largest and bloodiest anticolonial revolt against any European empire in the 19th century. Of the 139,000 sepoys of the Bengal army, all but 7,796 turned against the British. In many places the sepoys were supported by a widespread civilian rebellion.\\n\\nThere is much about British imperial adventures in the east at this time, and the massive insurgency it provoked, which is uneasily familiar to us today. The British had been trading in India since the early 17th century. But the commercial relationship changed towards the end of the 18th, as a new group of conservatives came to power in London, determined to make Britain the sole global power. Lord Wellesley, the brother of the Duke of Wellington and governor general in India from 1798 to 1805, called his new approach the Forward Policy. But it was in effect a project for a new British century. Wellesley made it clear he would not tolerate any European rivals, especially the French, and planned to remove any hostile Muslim regimes that might presume to resist the west's growing might.\\n\\nThe Forward Policy soon developed an evangelical flavour. The new conservatives wished to impose not only British laws but also western values on India. The country would be not only ruled but also redeemed. Local laws which offended Christian sensibilities were abrogated - the burning of widows, for instance, was banned. One of the East India Company directors, Charles Grant, spoke for many when he wrote of how he believed providence had brought the British to India for a higher purpose: \\"Is it not necessary to conclude that our Asiatic territories were given to us, not merely that we draw a profit from them, but that we might diffuse among their inhabitants, long sunk in darkness, the light of Truth?\\"\\n\\nThe British progressed from removing threatening Muslim rulers to annexing even the most pliant Islamic states. In February 1856, they marched into Avadh, also known by the British as Oudh. To support the annexation, a \\"dodgy dossier\\" was produced before parliament, so full of distortions and exaggerations that one British official who had been involved in the operation described the parliamentary blue book (or paper) on Oudh as \\"a fiction of official penmanship, an Oriental romance\\" that was refuted \\"by one simple and obstinate fact\\", that the conquered people of Avadh clearly \\"preferred the slandered regime\\" of the Nawab \\"to the grasping but rose-coloured government of the company\\".\\n\\nThe reaction to this came with the great mutiny, or as it is called in India, the first war of independence. Though it reflected many deeply held political and economic grievances, particularly the feeling that the heathen foreigners were interfering with a part of the world to which they were alien, the uprising was consistently articulated as a defensive action against the inroads missionaries and their ideas were making in India, combined with a generalised fight for freedom from western occupation.","question":"In what ways did the Indian Rebellion of 1857 challenge conventional narratives of British imperialism in India?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"By exposing the inherent contradictions within British colonial policies and practices.","B":"Through a concerted effort to integrate Indian traditions and values into British governance.","C":"By highlighting the mutual benefits of British-Indian cooperation and collaboration.","D":"Through the establishment of indigenous-led administrations independent of British influence."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"By exposing the inherent contradictions within British colonial policies and practices.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"B":"contradicts_passage","C":"contradicts_passage","D":"too_extreme"},"explanation":"Choice A is CORRECT — the rebellion exposed the gap between British claims of benevolent, civilising imperialism and the reality of coercive annexation, fabricated dossiers, and cultural imposition described in the passage. Choice B is incorrect — the rebellion was a resistance movement, not an effort to integrate Indian traditions into British governance; this contradicts the passage's description of the uprising. Choice C is incorrect — the passage describes the rebellion as a fight against British occupation, not a demonstration of mutual benefit; this contradicts the passage. Choice D is incorrect — while local leadership did emerge during the rebellion, the passage does not support the claim that independent indigenous administrations were durably established; this overstates what the passage describes.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E5_004","exercise":"exercise_5","passage_id":"RC_E5_P1","passage":"Soon after dawn on 11 May 1857, about 150 years ago, the Mughal Emperor Bahadur Shah Zafar was saying his morning prayers in his oratory overlooking the river Jumna when he saw a cloud of dust rising on the far side of the river. Minutes later, he was able to see its cause: 300 East India Company cavalrymen charging wildly towards his palace.\\n\\nThe troops had ridden overnight from Meerut, where they had turned their guns on their British officers and had come to Delhi to ask the emperor to give his blessing to their mutiny. As a letter sent out by the rebels' leaders subsequently put it: \\"The English are people who overthrow all religions ... As the English are the common enemy of both the Hindus and Muslims, we should unite in their slaughter ... By this alone will the lives and faiths of both be saved.\\"\\n\\nThe sepoys entered Delhi, massacred every Christian they could find, and declared the 82-year-old emperor to be their leader. Before long, the insurgency had snowballed into the largest and bloodiest anticolonial revolt against any European empire in the 19th century. Of the 139,000 sepoys of the Bengal army, all but 7,796 turned against the British. In many places the sepoys were supported by a widespread civilian rebellion.\\n\\nThere is much about British imperial adventures in the east at this time, and the massive insurgency it provoked, which is uneasily familiar to us today. The British had been trading in India since the early 17th century. But the commercial relationship changed towards the end of the 18th, as a new group of conservatives came to power in London, determined to make Britain the sole global power. Lord Wellesley, the brother of the Duke of Wellington and governor general in India from 1798 to 1805, called his new approach the Forward Policy. But it was in effect a project for a new British century. Wellesley made it clear he would not tolerate any European rivals, especially the French, and planned to remove any hostile Muslim regimes that might presume to resist the west's growing might.\\n\\nThe Forward Policy soon developed an evangelical flavour. The new conservatives wished to impose not only British laws but also western values on India. The country would be not only ruled but also redeemed. Local laws which offended Christian sensibilities were abrogated - the burning of widows, for instance, was banned. One of the East India Company directors, Charles Grant, spoke for many when he wrote of how he believed providence had brought the British to India for a higher purpose: \\"Is it not necessary to conclude that our Asiatic territories were given to us, not merely that we draw a profit from them, but that we might diffuse among their inhabitants, long sunk in darkness, the light of Truth?\\"\\n\\nThe British progressed from removing threatening Muslim rulers to annexing even the most pliant Islamic states. In February 1856, they marched into Avadh, also known by the British as Oudh. To support the annexation, a \\"dodgy dossier\\" was produced before parliament, so full of distortions and exaggerations that one British official who had been involved in the operation described the parliamentary blue book (or paper) on Oudh as \\"a fiction of official penmanship, an Oriental romance\\" that was refuted \\"by one simple and obstinate fact\\", that the conquered people of Avadh clearly \\"preferred the slandered regime\\" of the Nawab \\"to the grasping but rose-coloured government of the company\\".\\n\\nThe reaction to this came with the great mutiny, or as it is called in India, the first war of independence. Though it reflected many deeply held political and economic grievances, particularly the feeling that the heathen foreigners were interfering with a part of the world to which they were alien, the uprising was consistently articulated as a defensive action against the inroads missionaries and their ideas were making in India, combined with a generalised fight for freedom from western occupation.","question":"How did the rhetoric of \\"civilizing mission\\" influence British attitudes and policies towards India during the 19th century?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"It justified exploitative practices as necessary for the advancement of Indian society.","B":"By fostering a genuine desire to uplift and empower Indian communities through education and development.","C":"It served as a pretext for imposing Western cultural norms and values on Indian society.","D":"By promoting multiculturalism and respect for indigenous customs and traditions."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"It served as a pretext for imposing Western cultural norms and values on Indian society.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"correct_but_unstated","B":"contradicts_passage","D":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — while exploitation did occur under the guise of the civilising mission, the passage specifically frames the rhetoric as a pretext for cultural imposition rather than economic exploitation per se; this is a plausible but unstated framing in the relevant context. Choice B is incorrect — the passage presents the civilising mission as a self-serving ideology rather than a genuine uplift effort; Charles Grant's quote reveals paternalistic intent, not authentic empowerment; this contradicts the passage. Choice C is CORRECT — the passage explicitly states the Forward Policy developed an 'evangelical flavour' aimed at imposing 'not only British laws but also western values on India', making the civilising rhetoric a pretext for cultural imposition. Choice D is incorrect — the passage describes the abrogation of local laws and missionary inroads as the opposite of respecting indigenous customs; this contradicts the passage.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E5_005","exercise":"exercise_5","passage_id":"RC_E5_P1","passage":"Soon after dawn on 11 May 1857, about 150 years ago, the Mughal Emperor Bahadur Shah Zafar was saying his morning prayers in his oratory overlooking the river Jumna when he saw a cloud of dust rising on the far side of the river. Minutes later, he was able to see its cause: 300 East India Company cavalrymen charging wildly towards his palace.\\n\\nThe troops had ridden overnight from Meerut, where they had turned their guns on their British officers and had come to Delhi to ask the emperor to give his blessing to their mutiny. As a letter sent out by the rebels' leaders subsequently put it: \\"The English are people who overthrow all religions ... As the English are the common enemy of both the Hindus and Muslims, we should unite in their slaughter ... By this alone will the lives and faiths of both be saved.\\"\\n\\nThe sepoys entered Delhi, massacred every Christian they could find, and declared the 82-year-old emperor to be their leader. Before long, the insurgency had snowballed into the largest and bloodiest anticolonial revolt against any European empire in the 19th century. Of the 139,000 sepoys of the Bengal army, all but 7,796 turned against the British. In many places the sepoys were supported by a widespread civilian rebellion.\\n\\nThere is much about British imperial adventures in the east at this time, and the massive insurgency it provoked, which is uneasily familiar to us today. The British had been trading in India since the early 17th century. But the commercial relationship changed towards the end of the 18th, as a new group of conservatives came to power in London, determined to make Britain the sole global power. Lord Wellesley, the brother of the Duke of Wellington and governor general in India from 1798 to 1805, called his new approach the Forward Policy. But it was in effect a project for a new British century. Wellesley made it clear he would not tolerate any European rivals, especially the French, and planned to remove any hostile Muslim regimes that might presume to resist the west's growing might.\\n\\nThe Forward Policy soon developed an evangelical flavour. The new conservatives wished to impose not only British laws but also western values on India. The country would be not only ruled but also redeemed. Local laws which offended Christian sensibilities were abrogated - the burning of widows, for instance, was banned. One of the East India Company directors, Charles Grant, spoke for many when he wrote of how he believed providence had brought the British to India for a higher purpose: \\"Is it not necessary to conclude that our Asiatic territories were given to us, not merely that we draw a profit from them, but that we might diffuse among their inhabitants, long sunk in darkness, the light of Truth?\\"\\n\\nThe British progressed from removing threatening Muslim rulers to annexing even the most pliant Islamic states. In February 1856, they marched into Avadh, also known by the British as Oudh. To support the annexation, a \\"dodgy dossier\\" was produced before parliament, so full of distortions and exaggerations that one British official who had been involved in the operation described the parliamentary blue book (or paper) on Oudh as \\"a fiction of official penmanship, an Oriental romance\\" that was refuted \\"by one simple and obstinate fact\\", that the conquered people of Avadh clearly \\"preferred the slandered regime\\" of the Nawab \\"to the grasping but rose-coloured government of the company\\".\\n\\nThe reaction to this came with the great mutiny, or as it is called in India, the first war of independence. Though it reflected many deeply held political and economic grievances, particularly the feeling that the heathen foreigners were interfering with a part of the world to which they were alien, the uprising was consistently articulated as a defensive action against the inroads missionaries and their ideas were making in India, combined with a generalised fight for freedom from western occupation.","question":"What role did indigenous religious and cultural identities play in shaping resistance movements during the Indian Rebellion of 1857?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"They served as rallying points for disparate groups seeking to preserve their autonomy and heritage.","B":"By fostering unity and solidarity among diverse communities in the face of colonial oppression.","C":"Indigenous religious leaders actively collaborated with British authorities to suppress the rebellion.","D":"They were largely sidelined in favour of more secular and politically motivated forms of resistance."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"By fostering unity and solidarity among diverse communities in the face of colonial oppression.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"correct_but_unstated","C":"contradicts_passage","D":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — while this is broadly consistent with the passage, it focuses on autonomy and heritage preservation rather than the specific cross-community solidarity the passage emphasises; the rebel letter explicitly frames both Hindus and Muslims as united against a common enemy, making B the more precise answer. Choice B is CORRECT — the rebel letter quoted in the passage explicitly appeals to Hindu-Muslim unity against the British as 'the common enemy of both', showing religious and cultural identity fostering solidarity across communities. Choice C is incorrect — the passage gives no evidence of religious leaders siding with the British; in fact, the uprising is described as defensive action against missionary inroads, implying religious leaders opposed British authority. Choice D is incorrect — the passage consistently presents the uprising as 'articulated' through religious and cultural grievances, not sidelined by secular motives.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E5_006","exercise":"exercise_5","passage_id":"RC_E5_P2","passage":"Of the ten elements which remain permanently in gaseous form in the atmosphere, oxygen makes up about 21 per cent and nitrogen about 78 per cent. Several other gases, such as argon, carbon dioxide, hydrogen, neon, krypton, and xenon, comprise the remaining one percent of the volume of dry air. The amount of water vapour, and its variations in amount and distribution is extremely important in weather changes. Atmospheric gases hold in suspension great quantities of impurities which are always present in considerable, but variable amounts.\\n\\nThe atmosphere has no definite upper limits but gradually thins until it becomes imperceptible. Earlier assumption, was that the air above the first few miles gradually grew thinner and colder at a constant rate and that the upper air does not impact weather changes. Recent studies have disproved these assumptions. The atmosphere has three well-defined strata.\\n\\nThe layer of air next to the earth, which extends upward for about ten miles is known as the troposphere. Overall, it makes up about 75 per cent of all the weight of the atmosphere. It is the warmest part of the atmosphere because most of the solar radiation is absorbed by the earth's surface which warms the air immediately surrounding it. A steady decrease of temperature with increasing elevation is a most striking characteristic. The upper layers are colder because of their greater distance from the earth's surface and rapid radiation of heat into space. The temperature within the troposphere decreases about 3.5 degrees per 1000 feet increase in altitude. Within the troposphere, winds and air currents distribute heat and moisture. Strong winds, called jet streams, located at the upper levels of the troposphere, are both complex and widespread in occurrence. They move from west to east at velocities of 150 mph. The influences of changing locations and strengths of jet streams upon weather conditions and patterns are considerable.\\n\\nAbove the troposphere to a height of about 50 miles is the stratosphere, separated within the lower portions of the stratosphere is a layer of ozone gases which filters out most of the ultraviolet rays from the sun. If this zone were not there, the full blast of sun's ultraviolet light would burn our skins, blind our eyes, and eventually cause destruction. Within the stratosphere, the temperature and atmospheric composition are uniform.\\n\\nThe layer upward of about 50 miles is the most fascinating but the least known of these three strata. It is called the ionosphere because it consists of electrically charged particles called ions, thrown from the sun. The northern lights (aurora borealis) originates within this highly charged portion of the atmosphere. Its effect upon weather conditions, if any, is yet, unknown.","question":"Which of the following is the best title for the passage?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"The makeup of the atmosphere","B":"Studying the atmosphere","C":"Atmosphere and Weather","D":"Temperature in the stratosphere"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Atmosphere and Weather","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"correct_but_unstated","B":"out_of_scope","D":"irrelevant_comparison"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — while the passage does describe the composition of the atmosphere, it is primarily concerned with how atmospheric layers relate to weather; this is too narrow and covers only the first paragraph. Choice B is incorrect — the passage is not about the methodology of atmospheric study; this introduces a framing not present in the text. Choice C is CORRECT — the passage consistently links atmospheric structure (troposphere, stratosphere, ionosphere) to weather phenomena, making Atmosphere and Weather the most accurate and comprehensive title. Choice D is incorrect — temperature in the stratosphere is a minor detail in one paragraph, not the central focus of the passage.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E5_007","exercise":"exercise_5","passage_id":"RC_E5_P2","passage":"Of the ten elements which remain permanently in gaseous form in the atmosphere, oxygen makes up about 21 per cent and nitrogen about 78 per cent. Several other gases, such as argon, carbon dioxide, hydrogen, neon, krypton, and xenon, comprise the remaining one percent of the volume of dry air. The amount of water vapour, and its variations in amount and distribution is extremely important in weather changes. Atmospheric gases hold in suspension great quantities of impurities which are always present in considerable, but variable amounts.\\n\\nThe atmosphere has no definite upper limits but gradually thins until it becomes imperceptible. Earlier assumption, was that the air above the first few miles gradually grew thinner and colder at a constant rate and that the upper air does not impact weather changes. Recent studies have disproved these assumptions. The atmosphere has three well-defined strata.\\n\\nThe layer of air next to the earth, which extends upward for about ten miles is known as the troposphere. Overall, it makes up about 75 per cent of all the weight of the atmosphere. It is the warmest part of the atmosphere because most of the solar radiation is absorbed by the earth's surface which warms the air immediately surrounding it. A steady decrease of temperature with increasing elevation is a most striking characteristic. The upper layers are colder because of their greater distance from the earth's surface and rapid radiation of heat into space. The temperature within the troposphere decreases about 3.5 degrees per 1000 feet increase in altitude. Within the troposphere, winds and air currents distribute heat and moisture. Strong winds, called jet streams, located at the upper levels of the troposphere, are both complex and widespread in occurrence. They move from west to east at velocities of 150 mph. The influences of changing locations and strengths of jet streams upon weather conditions and patterns are considerable.\\n\\nAbove the troposphere to a height of about 50 miles is the stratosphere, separated within the lower portions of the stratosphere is a layer of ozone gases which filters out most of the ultraviolet rays from the sun. If this zone were not there, the full blast of sun's ultraviolet light would burn our skins, blind our eyes, and eventually cause destruction. Within the stratosphere, the temperature and atmospheric composition are uniform.\\n\\nThe layer upward of about 50 miles is the most fascinating but the least known of these three strata. It is called the ionosphere because it consists of electrically charged particles called ions, thrown from the sun. The northern lights (aurora borealis) originates within this highly charged portion of the atmosphere. Its effect upon weather conditions, if any, is yet, unknown.","question":"According to the passage, life as we know it exists on the earth because the atmosphere","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"contains a layer of ozone.","B":"contains electrically charged particles.","C":"is the warmest at the bottom.","D":"becomes thinner as we go higher up."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"contains a layer of ozone.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"B":"irrelevant_comparison","C":"correct_but_unstated","D":"correct_but_unstated"},"explanation":"Choice A is CORRECT — the passage explicitly states that without the ozone layer 'the full blast of sun's ultraviolet light would burn our skins, blind our eyes, and eventually cause destruction', establishing the ozone layer as essential to life as we know it. Choice B is incorrect — electrically charged particles are in the ionosphere and the passage explicitly states their effect on weather is 'yet unknown'; they are not linked to life existing on earth. Choice C is incorrect — while true that the troposphere is warmest at the bottom, the passage does not link this directly to life existing on earth; this is a factual detail without the causal connection the question requires. Choice D is incorrect — the thinning of the atmosphere with altitude is a structural fact stated in the passage but is not cited as the reason life exists on earth.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E5_008","exercise":"exercise_5","passage_id":"RC_E5_P2","passage":"Of the ten elements which remain permanently in gaseous form in the atmosphere, oxygen makes up about 21 per cent and nitrogen about 78 per cent. Several other gases, such as argon, carbon dioxide, hydrogen, neon, krypton, and xenon, comprise the remaining one percent of the volume of dry air. The amount of water vapour, and its variations in amount and distribution is extremely important in weather changes. Atmospheric gases hold in suspension great quantities of impurities which are always present in considerable, but variable amounts.\\n\\nThe atmosphere has no definite upper limits but gradually thins until it becomes imperceptible. Earlier assumption, was that the air above the first few miles gradually grew thinner and colder at a constant rate and that the upper air does not impact weather changes. Recent studies have disproved these assumptions. The atmosphere has three well-defined strata.\\n\\nThe layer of air next to the earth, which extends upward for about ten miles is known as the troposphere. Overall, it makes up about 75 per cent of all the weight of the atmosphere. It is the warmest part of the atmosphere because most of the solar radiation is absorbed by the earth's surface which warms the air immediately surrounding it. A steady decrease of temperature with increasing elevation is a most striking characteristic. The upper layers are colder because of their greater distance from the earth's surface and rapid radiation of heat into space. The temperature within the troposphere decreases about 3.5 degrees per 1000 feet increase in altitude. Within the troposphere, winds and air currents distribute heat and moisture. Strong winds, called jet streams, located at the upper levels of the troposphere, are both complex and widespread in occurrence. They move from west to east at velocities of 150 mph. The influences of changing locations and strengths of jet streams upon weather conditions and patterns are considerable.\\n\\nAbove the troposphere to a height of about 50 miles is the stratosphere, separated within the lower portions of the stratosphere is a layer of ozone gases which filters out most of the ultraviolet rays from the sun. If this zone were not there, the full blast of sun's ultraviolet light would burn our skins, blind our eyes, and eventually cause destruction. Within the stratosphere, the temperature and atmospheric composition are uniform.\\n\\nThe layer upward of about 50 miles is the most fascinating but the least known of these three strata. It is called the ionosphere because it consists of electrically charged particles called ions, thrown from the sun. The northern lights (aurora borealis) originates within this highly charged portion of the atmosphere. Its effect upon weather conditions, if any, is yet, unknown.","question":"Jet streams, according to the passage","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"are a flow of exhaust gases from a jet plane.","B":"can be felt just below the troposphere.","C":"are strong air currents which move from west to east.","D":"are all of the above"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"are strong air currents which move from west to east.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"contradicts_passage","D":"too_extreme"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — jet streams are atmospheric wind phenomena; associating them with jet plane exhaust is an out-of-scope inference based on the word 'jet'. Choice B is incorrect — the passage states jet streams are located 'at the upper levels of the troposphere', not below it; this contradicts the passage. Choice C is CORRECT — the passage states jet streams are 'strong winds' that 'move from west to east at velocities of 150 mph', located in the upper troposphere. Choice D is incorrect — since A and B are both wrong, 'all of the above' cannot be correct; students picking this fail to verify each component.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E5_009","exercise":"exercise_5","passage_id":"RC_E5_P2","passage":"Of the ten elements which remain permanently in gaseous form in the atmosphere, oxygen makes up about 21 per cent and nitrogen about 78 per cent. Several other gases, such as argon, carbon dioxide, hydrogen, neon, krypton, and xenon, comprise the remaining one percent of the volume of dry air. The amount of water vapour, and its variations in amount and distribution is extremely important in weather changes. Atmospheric gases hold in suspension great quantities of impurities which are always present in considerable, but variable amounts.\\n\\nThe atmosphere has no definite upper limits but gradually thins until it becomes imperceptible. Earlier assumption, was that the air above the first few miles gradually grew thinner and colder at a constant rate and that the upper air does not impact weather changes. Recent studies have disproved these assumptions. The atmosphere has three well-defined strata.\\n\\nThe layer of air next to the earth, which extends upward for about ten miles is known as the troposphere. Overall, it makes up about 75 per cent of all the weight of the atmosphere. It is the warmest part of the atmosphere because most of the solar radiation is absorbed by the earth's surface which warms the air immediately surrounding it. A steady decrease of temperature with increasing elevation is a most striking characteristic. The upper layers are colder because of their greater distance from the earth's surface and rapid radiation of heat into space. The temperature within the troposphere decreases about 3.5 degrees per 1000 feet increase in altitude. Within the troposphere, winds and air currents distribute heat and moisture. Strong winds, called jet streams, located at the upper levels of the troposphere, are both complex and widespread in occurrence. They move from west to east at velocities of 150 mph. The influences of changing locations and strengths of jet streams upon weather conditions and patterns are considerable.\\n\\nAbove the troposphere to a height of about 50 miles is the stratosphere, separated within the lower portions of the stratosphere is a layer of ozone gases which filters out most of the ultraviolet rays from the sun. If this zone were not there, the full blast of sun's ultraviolet light would burn our skins, blind our eyes, and eventually cause destruction. Within the stratosphere, the temperature and atmospheric composition are uniform.\\n\\nThe layer upward of about 50 miles is the most fascinating but the least known of these three strata. It is called the ionosphere because it consists of electrically charged particles called ions, thrown from the sun. The northern lights (aurora borealis) originates within this highly charged portion of the atmosphere. Its effect upon weather conditions, if any, is yet, unknown.","question":"Pick the odd man out.","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Stratosphere","B":"Lithosphere","C":"Ionosphere","D":"Troposphere"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Lithosphere","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","C":"premise_restatement","D":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — the stratosphere is explicitly discussed in the passage as one of the three atmospheric strata. Choice B is CORRECT — the lithosphere refers to the earth's crust and is not mentioned anywhere in the passage; it is not a layer of the atmosphere. Choice C is incorrect — the ionosphere is explicitly discussed as the third atmospheric stratum. Choice D is incorrect — the troposphere is extensively discussed as the lowest and most weather-relevant atmospheric layer. Students who pick A, C, or D are restating elements that are clearly present in the passage.","time_benchmark_seconds":60},{"id":"RC_E5_010","exercise":"exercise_5","passage_id":"RC_E5_P2","passage":"Of the ten elements which remain permanently in gaseous form in the atmosphere, oxygen makes up about 21 per cent and nitrogen about 78 per cent. Several other gases, such as argon, carbon dioxide, hydrogen, neon, krypton, and xenon, comprise the remaining one percent of the volume of dry air. The amount of water vapour, and its variations in amount and distribution is extremely important in weather changes. Atmospheric gases hold in suspension great quantities of impurities which are always present in considerable, but variable amounts.\\n\\nThe atmosphere has no definite upper limits but gradually thins until it becomes imperceptible. Earlier assumption, was that the air above the first few miles gradually grew thinner and colder at a constant rate and that the upper air does not impact weather changes. Recent studies have disproved these assumptions. The atmosphere has three well-defined strata.\\n\\nThe layer of air next to the earth, which extends upward for about ten miles is known as the troposphere. Overall, it makes up about 75 per cent of all the weight of the atmosphere. It is the warmest part of the atmosphere because most of the solar radiation is absorbed by the earth's surface which warms the air immediately surrounding it. A steady decrease of temperature with increasing elevation is a most striking characteristic. The upper layers are colder because of their greater distance from the earth's surface and rapid radiation of heat into space. The temperature within the troposphere decreases about 3.5 degrees per 1000 feet increase in altitude. Within the troposphere, winds and air currents distribute heat and moisture. Strong winds, called jet streams, located at the upper levels of the troposphere, are both complex and widespread in occurrence. They move from west to east at velocities of 150 mph. The influences of changing locations and strengths of jet streams upon weather conditions and patterns are considerable.\\n\\nAbove the troposphere to a height of about 50 miles is the stratosphere, separated within the lower portions of the stratosphere is a layer of ozone gases which filters out most of the ultraviolet rays from the sun. If this zone were not there, the full blast of sun's ultraviolet light would burn our skins, blind our eyes, and eventually cause destruction. Within the stratosphere, the temperature and atmospheric composition are uniform.\\n\\nThe layer upward of about 50 miles is the most fascinating but the least known of these three strata. It is called the ionosphere because it consists of electrically charged particles called ions, thrown from the sun. The northern lights (aurora borealis) originates within this highly charged portion of the atmosphere. Its effect upon weather conditions, if any, is yet, unknown.","question":"The passage states that the troposphere is the warmest part of the atmosphere because it","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"is closest to the sun.","B":"contains electrically charged particles.","C":"radiates heat into space.","D":"is warmed by the earth's heat."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"is warmed by the earth's heat.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"confuses_inference_with_assumption","B":"irrelevant_comparison","C":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — proximity to the sun is a plausible-sounding but incorrect inference; the passage explicitly states the troposphere is warm because the earth's surface absorbs solar radiation and warms the surrounding air, not because of direct proximity to the sun. Choice B is incorrect — electrically charged particles are in the ionosphere, not the troposphere; this confuses two different atmospheric layers. Choice C is incorrect — the passage states that upper layers radiate heat into space, making them colder, not warmer; applying this to the troposphere as a whole contradicts the passage. Choice D is CORRECT — the passage states 'most of the solar radiation is absorbed by the earth's surface which warms the air immediately surrounding it', identifying earth's surface heat as the source of the troposphere's warmth.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E5_011","exercise":"exercise_5","passage_id":"RC_E5_P2","passage":"Of the ten elements which remain permanently in gaseous form in the atmosphere, oxygen makes up about 21 per cent and nitrogen about 78 per cent. Several other gases, such as argon, carbon dioxide, hydrogen, neon, krypton, and xenon, comprise the remaining one percent of the volume of dry air. The amount of water vapour, and its variations in amount and distribution is extremely important in weather changes. Atmospheric gases hold in suspension great quantities of impurities which are always present in considerable, but variable amounts.\\n\\nThe atmosphere has no definite upper limits but gradually thins until it becomes imperceptible. Earlier assumption, was that the air above the first few miles gradually grew thinner and colder at a constant rate and that the upper air does not impact weather changes. Recent studies have disproved these assumptions. The atmosphere has three well-defined strata.\\n\\nThe layer of air next to the earth, which extends upward for about ten miles is known as the troposphere. Overall, it makes up about 75 per cent of all the weight of the atmosphere. It is the warmest part of the atmosphere because most of the solar radiation is absorbed by the earth's surface which warms the air immediately surrounding it. A steady decrease of temperature with increasing elevation is a most striking characteristic. The upper layers are colder because of their greater distance from the earth's surface and rapid radiation of heat into space. The temperature within the troposphere decreases about 3.5 degrees per 1000 feet increase in altitude. Within the troposphere, winds and air currents distribute heat and moisture. Strong winds, called jet streams, located at the upper levels of the troposphere, are both complex and widespread in occurrence. They move from west to east at velocities of 150 mph. The influences of changing locations and strengths of jet streams upon weather conditions and patterns are considerable.\\n\\nAbove the troposphere to a height of about 50 miles is the stratosphere, separated within the lower portions of the stratosphere is a layer of ozone gases which filters out most of the ultraviolet rays from the sun. If this zone were not there, the full blast of sun's ultraviolet light would burn our skins, blind our eyes, and eventually cause destruction. Within the stratosphere, the temperature and atmospheric composition are uniform.\\n\\nThe layer upward of about 50 miles is the most fascinating but the least known of these three strata. It is called the ionosphere because it consists of electrically charged particles called ions, thrown from the sun. The northern lights (aurora borealis) originates within this highly charged portion of the atmosphere. Its effect upon weather conditions, if any, is yet, unknown.","question":"According to the passage, the atmosphere consists of all the following except","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"21% oxygen.","B":"a definite amount of water vapour.","C":"10 permanent elements.","D":"considerable waste products."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"a definite amount of water vapour.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","C":"premise_restatement","D":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — the passage explicitly states 'oxygen makes up about 21 per cent'; this is directly stated. Choice B is CORRECT — the passage states 'The amount of water vapour, and its variations in amount and distribution is extremely important in weather changes', implying water vapour is variable, not present in a definite amount; 'a definite amount' is a distortion of the passage. Choice C is incorrect — the passage explicitly mentions 'ten elements which remain permanently in gaseous form in the atmosphere'. Choice D is incorrect — the passage states 'atmospheric gases hold in suspension great quantities of impurities', validating the presence of considerable waste products. Students who pick A, C or D are choosing options that are clearly stated in the passage.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E5_012","exercise":"exercise_5","passage_id":"RC_E5_P3","passage":"The cognitive approach of our behaviour is concerned with thinking, and the mental processes related to it. Unlike the behaviourist approach, the cognitive approach sees events within the person as being at least as important as the environmental stimuli in understanding behaviour, these events within the individual are described as mediators, because they come between the stimulus and the response.\\n\\nThe emphasis on mediating processes, and the way they are defined, is central to the cognitive approach. Cognitive psychologists, in contrast to the behaviourists believe that one cannot explain behaviour without reference to something more than stimulus-response connections. However, the nature of these mediators is also important. In essence, mediational processes are defined functionally, with reference to how behaviour is altered.\\n\\nThe development of the cognitive approach is closely related to behaviourism, since it developed as a reaction against the behaviourists' emphasis on external events. By the time Watson published the first edition of Behaviourism, he felt that his approach was gaining ground against the ambiguities of introspections. Yet at virtually the same point in time, the seeds for a new alternative were being sown. A German researcher named Wolfgang Kohler in his book called 'The Mentality of Apes' reported observations which suggested that animals could show insightful behaviour, and he rejected behaviourism in favour of an approach called 'Gestalt' psychology. The other challenge to behaviourism came from a behaviourist called E.C. Tolman, who published a book entitled 'Purposive Behaviour in Animals and Man', in which, he described research which was difficult to explain in terms of traditional behaviourism, which emphasized associations between stimuli and responses. Instead, Tolman talked about learning as based on relationships among stimuli, referred to as forming cognitive maps. He also argued that learning and responding are not the same, and that it is possible to learn without showing a correct response. Taken together, the work of Kohler and Tolman raised basic questions about the validity of behaviourism and laid the foundations for cognitive approach.\\n\\nWhile the early behaviourists saw learning as basically a matter of trial and error, Kohler argued that we tend to organize experience in particular ways. This is illustrated by the phenomenon Kohler called insight, which is a sudden change in the way one organizes a problem situation; typically this is characterized by a change in behaviour from random responding to rule-based responding.\\n\\nA famous example of Kohler's work on insight involved an ape named Sultan. Kohler gave Sultan a series of related problems, in which he had to use a stick to reach for a banana which was placed outside his cage, out of arm's reach. After solving such problems on several occasions, Sultan was then presented with a stick that was not long enough to reach the banana. However, outside the cage was a longer stick. After several unsuccessful tries, Sultan threw down the short stick in evident frustration and retreated to the corner of his cage. A little later, he suddenly went over to the short stick used it to reach the other, longer stick, and with the new stick reached the banana! As Kohler noted, there was no gradual sequence, as one might expect with shaping, nor did it seem to be the result of trial and error. Instead, there was a sudden transformation in the way Sultan organized the elements – insight. Kohler's work thus created a shift away from seeing all behaviour as trial and error, towards a concern with the internal organizing processes which mediate behaviour.","question":"Who laid the foundation for the cognitive approach?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Gestalt","B":"Watson","C":"Kohler and Tolman","D":"Sultan"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Kohler and Tolman","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"confuses_inference_with_assumption","B":"contradicts_passage","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — Gestalt is the approach that Kohler favoured, not a person; confusing the approach with the founder is a misreading of the passage. Choice B is incorrect — Watson developed behaviourism, which the cognitive approach reacted against; the passage explicitly states it was Kohler and Tolman who laid the foundations for the cognitive approach, not Watson. Choice C is CORRECT — the passage explicitly states 'Taken together, the work of Kohler and Tolman raised basic questions about the validity of behaviourism and laid the foundations for cognitive approach.' Choice D is incorrect — Sultan was the ape in Kohler's experiment, not a theorist; this is a distracting detail from the passage.","time_benchmark_seconds":60},{"id":"RC_E5_013","exercise":"exercise_5","passage_id":"RC_E5_P3","passage":"The cognitive approach of our behaviour is concerned with thinking, and the mental processes related to it. Unlike the behaviourist approach, the cognitive approach sees events within the person as being at least as important as the environmental stimuli in understanding behaviour, these events within the individual are described as mediators, because they come between the stimulus and the response.\\n\\nThe emphasis on mediating processes, and the way they are defined, is central to the cognitive approach. Cognitive psychologists, in contrast to the behaviourists believe that one cannot explain behaviour without reference to something more than stimulus-response connections. However, the nature of these mediators is also important. In essence, mediational processes are defined functionally, with reference to how behaviour is altered.\\n\\nThe development of the cognitive approach is closely related to behaviourism, since it developed as a reaction against the behaviourists' emphasis on external events. By the time Watson published the first edition of Behaviourism, he felt that his approach was gaining ground against the ambiguities of introspections. Yet at virtually the same point in time, the seeds for a new alternative were being sown. A German researcher named Wolfgang Kohler in his book called 'The Mentality of Apes' reported observations which suggested that animals could show insightful behaviour, and he rejected behaviourism in favour of an approach called 'Gestalt' psychology. The other challenge to behaviourism came from a behaviourist called E.C. Tolman, who published a book entitled 'Purposive Behaviour in Animals and Man', in which, he described research which was difficult to explain in terms of traditional behaviourism, which emphasized associations between stimuli and responses. Instead, Tolman talked about learning as based on relationships among stimuli, referred to as forming cognitive maps. He also argued that learning and responding are not the same, and that it is possible to learn without showing a correct response. Taken together, the work of Kohler and Tolman raised basic questions about the validity of behaviourism and laid the foundations for cognitive approach.\\n\\nWhile the early behaviourists saw learning as basically a matter of trial and error, Kohler argued that we tend to organize experience in particular ways. This is illustrated by the phenomenon Kohler called insight, which is a sudden change in the way one organizes a problem situation; typically this is characterized by a change in behaviour from random responding to rule-based responding.\\n\\nA famous example of Kohler's work on insight involved an ape named Sultan. Kohler gave Sultan a series of related problems, in which he had to use a stick to reach for a banana which was placed outside his cage, out of arm's reach. After solving such problems on several occasions, Sultan was then presented with a stick that was not long enough to reach the banana. However, outside the cage was a longer stick. After several unsuccessful tries, Sultan threw down the short stick in evident frustration and retreated to the corner of his cage. A little later, he suddenly went over to the short stick used it to reach the other, longer stick, and with the new stick reached the banana! As Kohler noted, there was no gradual sequence, as one might expect with shaping, nor did it seem to be the result of trial and error. Instead, there was a sudden transformation in the way Sultan organized the elements – insight. Kohler's work thus created a shift away from seeing all behaviour as trial and error, towards a concern with the internal organizing processes which mediate behaviour.","question":"The cognitive approach differed from the behaviourist approach in all of the following EXCEPT:","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Believing that there is a process that comes between stimulus and response.","B":"Deriving their conclusions from practical experiments.","C":"Looking at the mediation process as conceptual rather than physiological.","D":"Not relying only on external events."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Deriving their conclusions from practical experiments.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","C":"premise_restatement","D":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — this is an explicitly stated difference; the cognitive approach introduces mediators between stimulus and response, whereas behaviourism focuses on direct stimulus-response connections. Choice B is CORRECT — the passage makes no claim that behaviourists did not use practical experiments; both approaches drew on experimental observations (Watson's behaviourism, Kohler's ape experiments, Tolman's research). The use of practical experiments is not cited as a differentiating factor. Choice C is incorrect — the passage implies that mediational processes in the cognitive approach are functionally defined and conceptual, contrasting with behaviourism's purely external focus; this is a stated difference. Choice D is incorrect — the passage explicitly states the cognitive approach 'sees events within the person as being at least as important as the environmental stimuli', directly contrasting with behaviourism's reliance on external events.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E5_014","exercise":"exercise_5","passage_id":"RC_E5_P3","passage":"The cognitive approach of our behaviour is concerned with thinking, and the mental processes related to it. Unlike the behaviourist approach, the cognitive approach sees events within the person as being at least as important as the environmental stimuli in understanding behaviour, these events within the individual are described as mediators, because they come between the stimulus and the response.\\n\\nThe emphasis on mediating processes, and the way they are defined, is central to the cognitive approach. Cognitive psychologists, in contrast to the behaviourists believe that one cannot explain behaviour without reference to something more than stimulus-response connections. However, the nature of these mediators is also important. In essence, mediational processes are defined functionally, with reference to how behaviour is altered.\\n\\nThe development of the cognitive approach is closely related to behaviourism, since it developed as a reaction against the behaviourists' emphasis on external events. By the time Watson published the first edition of Behaviourism, he felt that his approach was gaining ground against the ambiguities of introspections. Yet at virtually the same point in time, the seeds for a new alternative were being sown. A German researcher named Wolfgang Kohler in his book called 'The Mentality of Apes' reported observations which suggested that animals could show insightful behaviour, and he rejected behaviourism in favour of an approach called 'Gestalt' psychology. The other challenge to behaviourism came from a behaviourist called E.C. Tolman, who published a book entitled 'Purposive Behaviour in Animals and Man', in which, he described research which was difficult to explain in terms of traditional behaviourism, which emphasized associations between stimuli and responses. Instead, Tolman talked about learning as based on relationships among stimuli, referred to as forming cognitive maps. He also argued that learning and responding are not the same, and that it is possible to learn without showing a correct response. Taken together, the work of Kohler and Tolman raised basic questions about the validity of behaviourism and laid the foundations for cognitive approach.\\n\\nWhile the early behaviourists saw learning as basically a matter of trial and error, Kohler argued that we tend to organize experience in particular ways. This is illustrated by the phenomenon Kohler called insight, which is a sudden change in the way one organizes a problem situation; typically this is characterized by a change in behaviour from random responding to rule-based responding.\\n\\nA famous example of Kohler's work on insight involved an ape named Sultan. Kohler gave Sultan a series of related problems, in which he had to use a stick to reach for a banana which was placed outside his cage, out of arm's reach. After solving such problems on several occasions, Sultan was then presented with a stick that was not long enough to reach the banana. However, outside the cage was a longer stick. After several unsuccessful tries, Sultan threw down the short stick in evident frustration and retreated to the corner of his cage. A little later, he suddenly went over to the short stick used it to reach the other, longer stick, and with the new stick reached the banana! As Kohler noted, there was no gradual sequence, as one might expect with shaping, nor did it seem to be the result of trial and error. Instead, there was a sudden transformation in the way Sultan organized the elements – insight. Kohler's work thus created a shift away from seeing all behaviour as trial and error, towards a concern with the internal organizing processes which mediate behaviour.","question":"Kohler's experiment with the apes showed that","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"apes are capable of learning from trial and error.","B":"unsuccessful attempts eventually lead one to success.","C":"apes can think and learn as much as humans.","D":"there was a sudden transformation in organizing elements that can be called insight."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"there was a sudden transformation in organizing elements that can be called insight.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","B":"correct_but_unstated","C":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — the passage explicitly states the experiment showed the opposite: Sultan's solution was not trial and error but a sudden insight; this contradicts the passage. Choice B is incorrect — while Sultan did succeed after unsuccessful attempts, this is not what the experiment was designed to show or what the passage emphasises as its conclusion; it is a surface-level reading of the narrative. Choice C is incorrect — the passage does not make a comparative claim about ape versus human cognition; this goes beyond what the passage states. Choice D is CORRECT — the passage explicitly concludes 'there was a sudden transformation in the way Sultan organized the elements – insight', which is the direct takeaway from the experiment.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E5_015","exercise":"exercise_5","passage_id":"RC_E5_P3","passage":"The cognitive approach of our behaviour is concerned with thinking, and the mental processes related to it. Unlike the behaviourist approach, the cognitive approach sees events within the person as being at least as important as the environmental stimuli in understanding behaviour, these events within the individual are described as mediators, because they come between the stimulus and the response.\\n\\nThe emphasis on mediating processes, and the way they are defined, is central to the cognitive approach. Cognitive psychologists, in contrast to the behaviourists believe that one cannot explain behaviour without reference to something more than stimulus-response connections. However, the nature of these mediators is also important. In essence, mediational processes are defined functionally, with reference to how behaviour is altered.\\n\\nThe development of the cognitive approach is closely related to behaviourism, since it developed as a reaction against the behaviourists' emphasis on external events. By the time Watson published the first edition of Behaviourism, he felt that his approach was gaining ground against the ambiguities of introspections. Yet at virtually the same point in time, the seeds for a new alternative were being sown. A German researcher named Wolfgang Kohler in his book called 'The Mentality of Apes' reported observations which suggested that animals could show insightful behaviour, and he rejected behaviourism in favour of an approach called 'Gestalt' psychology. The other challenge to behaviourism came from a behaviourist called E.C. Tolman, who published a book entitled 'Purposive Behaviour in Animals and Man', in which, he described research which was difficult to explain in terms of traditional behaviourism, which emphasized associations between stimuli and responses. Instead, Tolman talked about learning as based on relationships among stimuli, referred to as forming cognitive maps. He also argued that learning and responding are not the same, and that it is possible to learn without showing a correct response. Taken together, the work of Kohler and Tolman raised basic questions about the validity of behaviourism and laid the foundations for cognitive approach.\\n\\nWhile the early behaviourists saw learning as basically a matter of trial and error, Kohler argued that we tend to organize experience in particular ways. This is illustrated by the phenomenon Kohler called insight, which is a sudden change in the way one organizes a problem situation; typically this is characterized by a change in behaviour from random responding to rule-based responding.\\n\\nA famous example of Kohler's work on insight involved an ape named Sultan. Kohler gave Sultan a series of related problems, in which he had to use a stick to reach for a banana which was placed outside his cage, out of arm's reach. After solving such problems on several occasions, Sultan was then presented with a stick that was not long enough to reach the banana. However, outside the cage was a longer stick. After several unsuccessful tries, Sultan threw down the short stick in evident frustration and retreated to the corner of his cage. A little later, he suddenly went over to the short stick used it to reach the other, longer stick, and with the new stick reached the banana! As Kohler noted, there was no gradual sequence, as one might expect with shaping, nor did it seem to be the result of trial and error. Instead, there was a sudden transformation in the way Sultan organized the elements – insight. Kohler's work thus created a shift away from seeing all behaviour as trial and error, towards a concern with the internal organizing processes which mediate behaviour.","question":"Thinking rightly belongs to","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"cognitivism.","B":"traditional behaviourism.","C":"biological approach.","D":"None of the above"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"cognitivism.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"B":"contradicts_passage","C":"out_of_scope","D":"too_extreme"},"explanation":"Choice A is CORRECT — the passage opens by stating 'The cognitive approach of our behaviour is concerned with thinking, and the mental processes related to it', directly attributing thinking to cognitivism. Choice B is incorrect — behaviourism focuses on external stimuli and responses, explicitly contrasted in the passage with the cognitive approach's concern with internal mental processes; thinking belongs to cognitivism, not behaviourism. Choice C is incorrect — the biological approach is not mentioned in the passage; this is an out-of-scope distractor. Choice D is incorrect — since cognitivism is explicitly defined around thinking in the passage, 'none of the above' overclaims by dismissing a directly stated answer.","time_benchmark_seconds":60},{"id":"RC_E5_016","exercise":"exercise_5","passage_id":"RC_E5_P4","passage":"Most political reformers of the past agree that authoritarian rule and an excessive concentration of power hinder social and individual progress. The goal of those who wish to change the society for the better is freedom, justice and peaceful co-operation between responsible individuals. Is there the smallest reason to believe that such a goal can be reached through police spying, military slavery, the centralization of power, the suppression of free discussion and the imposition of an authoritarian system of education? Emphatically, the answer is No. In any given society, the state exists, among other reasons, for the purpose of ensuring to the ruling class the continuance of its privileges. Thus, in a feudal community, the state is the instrument by means of which the landlords keep themselves in power. Under capitalism, the state is the instrument by means of which the middle class or bourgeoisie retains its right to rule and to be rich. Similarly, under state socialism, the state is the instrument by means of which the ruling bureaucracy defends the position to which it has climbed. A highly centralized dictatorial state may be smashed by war or overturned by a revolution from below : but there is not the smallest reason to believe that it will just wither away. \\"Dictatorship of the proletariat\\" is dictatorship by a small, privileged minority; it does not lead to liberty, justice, peace, it leads to more dictatorship or to war, or to revolution.\\n\\nThe political road to a better society is the road of decentralization and responsible self-government. Now, a society which is preparing for war cannot afford to be anything but highly centralized. Unity of command is essential, before and after the outbreak of war. Hence the absurdity of talking of the defence of democracy by force of arms. A democracy which makes, or even effectively prepares for, modern, scientific war must necessarily cease to be democratic.\\n\\nNo country can be well prepared for modern war unless it is governed by a tyrant, at the head of a highly trained and perfectly obedient bureaucracy. Further, a country which possesses a highly centralized, all-powerful government is more likely to wage war than a country where power is decentralized and the people genuinely govern themselves.\\n\\nThus, we see that extreme centralization of power is not only necessary if war is to be waged successfully; it is also a contributory cause of war. So long as civilized countries continue to prepare for war, it is highly improbable that any of them will pursue a policy of decentralization and extension of the principle of self-government. The circumstances of our times conspire to create a general war psychosis. In such an atmosphere, there is no possibility of civilized countries pursuing a policy of decentralization and extension of self-government. On the contrary, power will tend to become more narrowly concentrated than at present, not only in the totalitarian countries but also in the democratic countries. Like the totalitarian countries, the democratic countries are spending huge sums of money on armaments. Armaments mean not only weapons and explosives but rigorously disciplined men to use them, and a submissive labour force to manufacture them.\\n\\nNow therefore, more than ever before, it is necessary for the \\"leftist\\" politicians in the democracies to see and clearly understand the forces at work and instead of hoping for \\"a firm stand\\" against the totalitarian countries, which would inevitably lead to war and consequent curtailment of liberties, strive to oppose the forces which are building up towards the catastrophe of war.","question":"How, according to the passage, are democratic countries trying to strengthen their power of self-defence?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"They are trying to inspire nationalist sentiment and promote economic imperialism.","B":"They are pursuing a policy of decentralization and extension of the principles of self-government.","C":"They are spending huge sums on the labour force to manufacture armaments and to train people to use them.","D":"They are making efforts to make power concentrated in a single governing body."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"They are spending huge sums on the labour force to manufacture armaments and to train people to use them.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"contradicts_passage","D":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — nationalist sentiment and economic imperialism are not mentioned in the passage as strategies democratic countries are pursuing; this introduces claims not in the text. Choice B is incorrect — the passage explicitly states that under war psychosis 'there is no possibility of civilized countries pursuing a policy of decentralization'; this directly contradicts the passage. Choice C is CORRECT — the passage states 'the democratic countries are spending huge sums of money on armaments. Armaments mean not only weapons and explosives but rigorously disciplined men to use them, and a submissive labour force to manufacture them.' Choice D is incorrect — while power concentration is a trend the passage describes, it is presented as an undesirable consequence of war preparation, not an active democratic policy effort; the passage does not say democracies are intentionally centralising into a single governing body.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E5_017","exercise":"exercise_5","passage_id":"RC_E5_P4","passage":"Most political reformers of the past agree that authoritarian rule and an excessive concentration of power hinder social and individual progress. The goal of those who wish to change the society for the better is freedom, justice and peaceful co-operation between responsible individuals. Is there the smallest reason to believe that such a goal can be reached through police spying, military slavery, the centralization of power, the suppression of free discussion and the imposition of an authoritarian system of education? Emphatically, the answer is No. In any given society, the state exists, among other reasons, for the purpose of ensuring to the ruling class the continuance of its privileges. Thus, in a feudal community, the state is the instrument by means of which the landlords keep themselves in power. Under capitalism, the state is the instrument by means of which the middle class or bourgeoisie retains its right to rule and to be rich. Similarly, under state socialism, the state is the instrument by means of which the ruling bureaucracy defends the position to which it has climbed. A highly centralized dictatorial state may be smashed by war or overturned by a revolution from below : but there is not the smallest reason to believe that it will just wither away. \\"Dictatorship of the proletariat\\" is dictatorship by a small, privileged minority; it does not lead to liberty, justice, peace, it leads to more dictatorship or to war, or to revolution.\\n\\nThe political road to a better society is the road of decentralization and responsible self-government. Now, a society which is preparing for war cannot afford to be anything but highly centralized. Unity of command is essential, before and after the outbreak of war. Hence the absurdity of talking of the defence of democracy by force of arms. A democracy which makes, or even effectively prepares for, modern, scientific war must necessarily cease to be democratic.\\n\\nNo country can be well prepared for modern war unless it is governed by a tyrant, at the head of a highly trained and perfectly obedient bureaucracy. Further, a country which possesses a highly centralized, all-powerful government is more likely to wage war than a country where power is decentralized and the people genuinely govern themselves.\\n\\nThus, we see that extreme centralization of power is not only necessary if war is to be waged successfully; it is also a contributory cause of war. So long as civilized countries continue to prepare for war, it is highly improbable that any of them will pursue a policy of decentralization and extension of the principle of self-government. The circumstances of our times conspire to create a general war psychosis. In such an atmosphere, there is no possibility of civilized countries pursuing a policy of decentralization and extension of self-government. On the contrary, power will tend to become more narrowly concentrated than at present, not only in the totalitarian countries but also in the democratic countries. Like the totalitarian countries, the democratic countries are spending huge sums of money on armaments. Armaments mean not only weapons and explosives but rigorously disciplined men to use them, and a submissive labour force to manufacture them.\\n\\nNow therefore, more than ever before, it is necessary for the \\"leftist\\" politicians in the democracies to see and clearly understand the forces at work and instead of hoping for \\"a firm stand\\" against the totalitarian countries, which would inevitably lead to war and consequent curtailment of liberties, strive to oppose the forces which are building up towards the catastrophe of war.","question":"How does state socialism describe the state?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"As an instrument that helps the middle class retain its right to rule and acquire property.","B":"As a means to ensure the continuation of privileges to the ruling class.","C":"As an instrument by which landlords keep themselves in power.","D":"As a means by which the bureaucrats in power defend their positions."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"As a means by which the bureaucrats in power defend their positions.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"irrelevant_comparison","B":"too_extreme","C":"irrelevant_comparison"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — this describes capitalism, not state socialism; students confuse the two systems described sequentially in the passage. Choice B is incorrect — this is a general description of the state's purpose across all systems, not the specific characterisation under state socialism; it is too broad and generalised. Choice C is incorrect — this describes the feudal state, not state socialism; students confuse the feudal characterisation with the socialist one. Choice D is CORRECT — the passage explicitly states 'under state socialism, the state is the instrument by means of which the ruling bureaucracy defends the position to which it has climbed.'","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E5_018","exercise":"exercise_5","passage_id":"RC_E5_P4","passage":"Most political reformers of the past agree that authoritarian rule and an excessive concentration of power hinder social and individual progress. The goal of those who wish to change the society for the better is freedom, justice and peaceful co-operation between responsible individuals. Is there the smallest reason to believe that such a goal can be reached through police spying, military slavery, the centralization of power, the suppression of free discussion and the imposition of an authoritarian system of education? Emphatically, the answer is No. In any given society, the state exists, among other reasons, for the purpose of ensuring to the ruling class the continuance of its privileges. Thus, in a feudal community, the state is the instrument by means of which the landlords keep themselves in power. Under capitalism, the state is the instrument by means of which the middle class or bourgeoisie retains its right to role and to be rich. Similarly, under state socialism, the state is the instrument by means of which the ruling bureaucracy defends the position to which it has climbed. A highly centralized dictatorial state may be smashed by war or overturned by a revolution from below : but there is not the smallest reason to believe that it will just wither away. \\"Dictatorship of the proletariat\\" is dictatorship by a small, privileged minority; it does not lead to liberty, justice, peace, it leads to more dictatorship or to war, or to revolution.\\n\\nThe political road to a better society is the road of decentralization and responsible self-government. Now, a society which is preparing for war cannot afford to be anything but highly centralized. Unity of command is essential, before and after the outbreak of war. Hence the absurdity of talking of the defence of democracy by force of arms. A democracy which makes, or even effectively prepares for, modern, scientific war must necessarily cease to be democratic.\\n\\nNo country can be well prepared for modern war unless it is governed by a tyrant, at the head of a highly trained and perfectly obedient bureaucracy. Further, a country which possesses a highly centralized, all-powerful government is more likely to wage war than a country where power is decentralized and the people genuinely govern themselves.\\n\\nThus, we see that extreme centralization of power is not only necessary if war is to be waged successfully; it is also a contributory cause of war. So long as civilized countries continue to prepare for war, it is highly improbable that any of them will pursue a policy of decentralization and extension of the principle of self-government. The circumstances of our times conspire to create a general war psychosis. In such an atmosphere, there is no possibility of civilized countries pursuing a policy of decentralization and extension of self-government. On the contrary, power will tend to become more narrowly concentrated than at present, not only in the totalitarian countries but also in the democratic countries. Like the totalitarian countries, the democratic countries are spending huge sums of money on armaments. Armaments mean not only weapons and explosives but rigorously disciplined men to use them, and a submissive labour force to manufacture them.\\n\\nNow therefore, more than ever before, it is necessary for the \\"leftist\\" politicians in the democracies to see and clearly understand the forces at work and instead of hoping for \\"a firm stand\\" against the totalitarian countries, which would inevitably lead to war and consequent curtailment of liberties, strive to oppose the forces which are building up towards the catastrophe of war.","question":"What would be the consequence of a \\"firm stand\\" against totalitarian countries?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"A war will have to be fought only with the use of intelligence.","B":"Politicians in the democracies will fail to understand the forces at work.","C":"Possibility of a war and liberty being at stake in democracies.","D":"People will come up with a new spirit of patriotism."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Possibility of a war and liberty being at stake in democracies.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"contradicts_passage","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — intelligence as a war instrument is not mentioned in the passage; this introduces a claim not in the text. Choice B is incorrect — the passage calls on leftist politicians to understand the forces at work precisely so they avoid a firm stand; a firm stand would not cause failure to understand, it would be the product of that failure; this inverts the passage's logic. Choice C is CORRECT — the passage explicitly states a firm stand 'would inevitably lead to war and consequent curtailment of liberties'. Choice D is incorrect — patriotism is mentioned in the passage only as something to be 'intelligently fought' against; the passage does not predict a new spirit of patriotism as a consequence of a firm stand.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E5_019","exercise":"exercise_5","passage_id":"RC_E5_P4","passage":"Most political reformers of the past agree that authoritarian rule and an excessive concentration of power hinder social and individual progress. The goal of those who wish to change the society for the better is freedom, justice and peaceful co-operation between responsible individuals. Is there the smallest reason to believe that such a goal can be reached through police spying, military slavery, the centralization of power, the suppression of free discussion and the imposition of an authoritarian system of education? Emphatically, the answer is No. In any given society, the state exists, among other reasons, for the purpose of ensuring to the ruling class the continuance of its privileges. Thus, in a feudal community, the state is the instrument by means of which the landlords keep themselves in power. Under capitalism, the state is the instrument by means of which the middle class or bourgeoisie retains its right to rule and to be rich. Similarly, under state socialism, the state is the instrument by means of which the ruling bureaucracy defends the position to which it has climbed. A highly centralized dictatorial state may be smashed by war or overturned by a revolution from below : but there is not the smallest reason to believe that it will just wither away. \\"Dictatorship of the proletariat\\" is dictatorship by a small, privileged minority; it does not lead to liberty, justice, peace, it leads to more dictatorship or to war, or to revolution.\\n\\nThe political road to a better society is the road of decentralization and responsible self-government. Now, a society which is preparing for war cannot afford to be anything but highly centralized. Unity of command is essential, before and after the outbreak of war. Hence the absurdity of talking of the defence of democracy by force of arms. A democracy which makes, or even effectively prepares for, modern, scientific war must necessarily cease to be democratic.\\n\\nNo country can be well prepared for modern war unless it is governed by a tyrant, at the head of a highly trained and perfectly obedient bureaucracy. Further, a country which possesses a highly centralized, all-powerful government is more likely to wage war than a country where power is decentralized and the people genuinely govern themselves.\\n\\nThus, we see that extreme centralization of power is not only necessary if war is to be waged successfully; it is also a contributory cause of war. So long as civilized countries continue to prepare for war, it is highly improbable that any of them will pursue a policy of decentralization and extension of the principle of self-government. The circumstances of our times conspire to create a general war psychosis. In such an atmosphere, there is no possibility of civilized countries pursuing a policy of decentralization and extension of self-government. On the contrary, power will tend to become more narrowly concentrated than at present, not only in the totalitarian countries but also in the democratic countries. Like the totalitarian countries, the democratic countries are spending huge sums of money on armaments. Armaments mean not only weapons and explosives but rigorously disciplined men to use them, and a submissive labour force to manufacture them.\\n\\nNow therefore, more than ever before, it is necessary for the \\"leftist\\" politicians in the democracies to see and clearly understand the forces at work and instead of hoping for \\"a firm stand\\" against the totalitarian countries, which would inevitably lead to war and consequent curtailment of liberties, strive to oppose the forces which are building up towards the catastrophe of war.","question":"What were the chief obstacles on the way to social and individual progress in the past?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Secret police systems.","B":"Dictatorship of the proletariat and communist ideologies.","C":"Authoritarian rule and excessive concentration of power.","D":"Increase in the powers of the Central Government and military conscription."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Authoritarian rule and excessive concentration of power.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"correct_but_unstated","B":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — police spying is listed as a method that cannot achieve progress, but is not explicitly cited as an obstacle to social and individual progress in the passage's opening framing; this is a related but imprecisely matched detail. Choice B is incorrect — while the dictatorship of the proletariat is criticised in the passage, 'communist ideologies' as a category is not cited as a chief obstacle; this overreaches what the passage states. Choice C is CORRECT — the very first sentence of the passage states 'Most political reformers of the past agree that authoritarian rule and an excessive concentration of power hinder social and individual progress.' Choice D is incorrect — increases in central government power and military conscription are consequences of war preparation, not identified as the chief historical obstacles to progress.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E5_020","exercise":"exercise_5","passage_id":"RC_E5_P4","passage":"Most political reformers of the past agree that authoritarian rule and an excessive concentration of power hinder social and individual progress. The goal of those who wish to change the society for the better is freedom, justice and peaceful co-operation between responsible individuals. Is there the smallest reason to believe that such a goal can be reached through police spying, military slavery, the centralization of power, the suppression of free discussion and the imposition of an authoritarian system of education? Emphatically, the answer is No. In any given society, the state exists, among other reasons, for the purpose of ensuring to the ruling class the continuance of its privileges. Thus, in a feudal community, the state is the instrument by means of which the landlords keep themselves in power. Under capitalism, the state is the instrument by means of which the middle class or bourgeoisie retains its right to rule and to be rich. Similarly, under state socialism, the state is the instrument by means of which the ruling bureaucracy defends the position to which it has climbed. A highly centralized dictatorial state may be smashed by war or overturned by a revolution from below : but there is not the smallest reason to believe that it will just wither away. \\"Dictatorship of the proletariat\\" is dictatorship by a small, privileged minority; it does not lead to liberty, justice, peace, it leads to more dictatorship or to war, or to revolution.\\n\\nThe political road to a better society is the road of decentralization and responsible self-government. Now, a society which is preparing for war cannot afford to be anything but highly centralized. Unity of command is essential, before and after the outbreak of war. Hence the absurdity of talking of the defence of democracy by force of arms. A democracy which makes, or even effectively prepares for, modern, scientific war must necessarily cease to be democratic.\\n\\nNo country can be well prepared for modern war unless it is governed by a tyrant, at the head of a highly trained and perfectly obedient bureaucracy. Further, a country which possesses a highly centralized, all-powerful government is more likely to wage war than a country where power is decentralized and the people genuinely govern themselves.\\n\\nThus, we see that extreme centralization of power is not only necessary if war is to be waged successfully; it is also a contributory cause of war. So long as civilized countries continue to prepare for war, it is highly improbable that any of them will pursue a policy of decentralization and extension of the principle of self-government. The circumstances of our times conspire to create a general war psychosis. In such an atmosphere, there is no possibility of civilized countries pursuing a policy of decentralization and extension of self-government. On the contrary, power will tend to become more narrowly concentrated than at present, not only in the totalitarian countries but also in the democratic countries. Like the totalitarian countries, the democratic countries are spending huge sums of money on armaments. Armaments mean not only weapons and explosives but rigorously disciplined men to use them, and a submissive labour force to manufacture them.\\n\\nNow therefore, more than ever before, it is necessary for the \\"leftist\\" politicians in the democracies to see and clearly understand the forces at work and instead of hoping for \\"a firm stand\\" against the totalitarian countries, which would inevitably lead to war and consequent curtailment of liberties, strive to oppose the forces which are building up towards the catastrophe of war.","question":"As understood from the passage, when is a country most likely to prepare for war?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"When there is a tyrant ruling over the country, with highly trained and obedient bureaucrats.","B":"When it has a perfect democracy equipped with arms required for a modern, scientific war.","C":"When it has its power decentralized and equally distributed among the sections of its governing body.","D":"When the power of command is not concentrated on any single body."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"When there is a tyrant ruling over the country, with highly trained and obedient bureaucrats.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"B":"contradicts_passage","C":"contradicts_passage","D":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"Choice A is CORRECT — the passage states 'No country can be well prepared for modern war unless it is governed by a tyrant, at the head of a highly trained and perfectly obedient bureaucracy', directly linking tyrannical centralised rule to war preparedness. Choice B is incorrect — the passage argues that a democracy preparing for modern war 'must necessarily cease to be democratic'; a perfect democracy and war preparation are presented as mutually exclusive, contradicting this option. Choice C is incorrect — the passage explicitly states that decentralisation is incompatible with war preparation; a country with decentralised power is less likely to wage war, not more, contradicting this option. Choice D is incorrect — the passage states unity of command is essential for war; absence of concentrated command contradicts the conditions for war preparation.","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]`);export{e as default};
